00:02:59.362 --> 00:03:02.400
to deliver positive social benefits.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.913
We're gonna start with Mo.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.910
- Okay yeah, thanks so much,
Elissa for the introduction

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.200
and for hosting the event.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.590
So I guess I can talk about
my research for a while

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.500
but maybe I'll give the short version.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.180
So my current research
aims to enable robots to

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.540
behave safely and naturally among people.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.160
So we work on topics such as

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.720
human intending prints,
collision avoidance

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.530
for mobile robots,
multi-agent coordination.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.180
So our techniques can be
applied to indoor places

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.240
like schools and stores
and also outdoors as well.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.090
So I think the key here is to be safe,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.290
for robots to be safe, but still useful.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.350
So of course a robot
can be completely safe

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:45.590
it, if it operates far from
people or a for example, a car

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.460
a self-driving car is just
staying parked, right?

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.170
But we need to like to
go further than that.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:52.460
We need to walk the fine line

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.350
between interacting closely with people

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:58.950
and still staying safe and
hopefully trust it eventually.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.987
- Yeah, so that robots can
eventually be our partners in

00:04:01.987 --> 00:04:04.300
much of the work that we do right?

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.133
- Right, right.

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:07.840
Yeah, yes, so maybe getting
a little bit more technical.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.520
So our methodology
involves control theory,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.750
reinforcement learning computer vision.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.080
So for example, we try to adapt

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.400
safety verification algorithms

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.410
that are used in the aerospace

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.080
to product environments on the ground.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.600
We also try to combine classical
optimization techniques

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.380
with data-driven learning based methods.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.380
So we have several examples of that.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.860
one of them I like to
bring up is like, we try to

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.170
use learning based methods to
do a higher level planning.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.560
Like where am I should
go next at a high level?

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.900
And then the details of
how to get there safely

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.090
can be taken care of by some
of these classical algorithms.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.876
Yeah, so I guess you asked the
second part of the question

00:04:49.876 --> 00:04:51.830
positive societal benefits?

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.070
Autonomous robots today

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.550
are often used in controlled
environments like warehouses

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.310
or maybe in the absence of other agents

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.020
like robot Watson people.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.590
So I, hopefully my research will allow us,

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.880
well our robots, to interact
with people more closely,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.020
for applications such as like maybe a

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.830
robot assistant, delivery
vehicles, autonomous cars.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:14.560
And for example, in one project

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.960
we put a collision
avoidance system on robots

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.280
and then we have students
try to drive a robot

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.470
into a wall or into obstacles
in unknown environments

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.270
in the wild they try, but they
fail to cause a collision.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.770
So that's a, that's a
really cool experiment.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.210
And we are, we're also working

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.740
on something called a follow ahead project

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.770
where a robot tries to autonomously

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.330
stay in front of a person.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.090
So kind of imagine you
have a shopping cart

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.240
but instead of you pushing a shopping cart

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.180
it's kind of just pushing
itself in front of you.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.100
So I think I'll stop here.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.650
- Those are some, some great examples

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.870
of fun graduate research
projects going on in your lab.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.570
Thanks for sharing those.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.660
Nidhi over to you, tell
us about your research

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.030
and how it's driving towards delivering

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.413
positive benefits for society.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.990
- Yeah, hi, Elissa thank
you for the introduction

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.650
and for hosting this
very interesting event.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:13.470
So my current research
is in looking at privacy

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.300
and fairness in machine learning.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.410
So what I'm interested in is looking at

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.930
real practical problems

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.210
where some of the current
privacy preserving methods

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.780
don't really fit very well,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.550
and what are the fundamentals
that we need to work

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.670
on in order to get that working?

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:31.720
So for example, a lot
of data is structured.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.800
For example, the data that
you're producing when you're

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.780
on social networks, which means, you know

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.110
a data point does not
necessarily independent

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:42.290
of the other data points
there's related somehow,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.750
and this could be for example,
in the structure of a graph.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:49.251
If we have apply standard
privacy preserving methods

00:06:49.251 --> 00:06:52.750
then this doesn't scale well to the size.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.860
So you can, we can't look
at large data sets anymore.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.860
The noise, the privacy
costs that you would need

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.240
and the compute power you would need

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.050
would quickly be prohibitive.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.980
So I'm looking at that kind of question.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.717
Another example is let's
say different partners

00:07:12.717 --> 00:07:16.693
in machine learning and
AI want to share data,

00:07:16.693 --> 00:07:19.410
but you know, we don't
want to share actual data

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.700
because of privacy concerns.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.270
So we might want to
create synthetic data sets

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.750
and share those.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.680
But synthetic data sets are
not private by themselves.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.330
So we need to do something
else to make them private.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:31.820
There are some methods out there

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.700
but they're very specific
to certain tasks.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.240
So how can we generalize this?

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.990
And another interesting problem is

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.490
how does privacy interplay with fairness?

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:46.490
Can we have both in a particular
machine learning model?

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.200
Some preliminary work has shown

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.800
that it's not possible for
all conditions to have both.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.580
So how do we make sure,

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:58.090
can we devise new algorithms
that make sure that

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:02.070
we can have a privacy preserving
outcome that is also fair?

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.960
- Your research really sort
of reminds me that, you know,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.068
we need to strike such a
fine balance between privacy

00:08:08.068 --> 00:08:10.380
and data access in order
to be able to do the

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.360
machine learning research

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.500
that we've been talking about today.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.960
Of course we need researchers,
need access to data

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.450
so that's why your work is so critical.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.130
Any other thoughts on the social benefits?

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:24.900
- Yeah, so obviously, you
know, by the definition

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.900
of the problems I'm
working at, it has obvious

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:31.210
social benefits, you
know, machine learning AI

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.210
really impacts every part of our lives.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:38.150
So anywhere that we can
limit damage or harm

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:42.450
like privacy breaches or
biased outcomes that lead

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.810
to decisions that impact
people in their daily lives

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.040
for instance is important.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:53.010
So, you know, this type of
work has a clear end goal,

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:57.540
which is to safeguard
People from, you know,

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.023
potential outcomes.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.170
- And impacts on all of us as individuals.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.003
Yeah, great.

00:09:03.003 --> 00:09:03.930
Thank you very much.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:06.910
Rahul, over to you, tell
us about your research

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:11.543
and it's opportunity for
positive social benefits.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.487
- Thanks so much.

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:19.010
So I, thank you again for
organizing this wonderful

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.080
set of talks and panels, my own research,

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.910
maybe I'll start with a
bit of historical context.

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.801
And so over the last three decades

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:33.300
there has been an increasing realization

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.190
that a lot of the healthcare data

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:39.036
that's been digitized by
hospitals as they went to record

00:09:39.036 --> 00:09:41.950
how patients interacted
with the healthcare system

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.930
can be of use beyond the original intent

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.850
that these records were created for.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:48.530
So originally a lot of
these healthcare records

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:49.780
were created for bookkeeping

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.730
and for billing purposes
across North America.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:55.150
And in the last few decades

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.870
what we've seen is that
people have been interested

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.820
in understanding how
the patterns that live

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.031
within these data can be used

00:10:03.031 --> 00:10:05.640
for the creation of
machine learning models

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.430
to automate clinically meaningful tasks.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.610
And so why might that be interesting?

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.770
Well, for one, we know
that access to health care

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.590
and the quality of healthcare access

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.500
that people do have is
not equally distributed.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.720
So part of the pitch for
this line of work is to

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:26.750
help transfer some of the
knowledge that may exist

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:29.570
in high resource settings
to low resource settings

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.880
via the use of predictive
models and machine learning.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.810
And so more broadly, my work
lies at this intersection

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.800
of machine learning and
healthcare, and within it

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.500
I do research both in applied areas

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:44.630
as well as more methodological areas.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:46.840
And so to give two concrete examples

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.170
of some of the applied work
that I do, one is on this idea

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.540
of patient risk
stratification, which is to say

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:54.370
can we use algorithms and models

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.517
for machine learning to find
patterns and patient data

00:10:57.517 --> 00:10:59.020
and predict whether or not

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.760
they will have adverse
outcomes in the future.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.002
Now at the outset, this looks

00:11:03.002 --> 00:11:07.030
like a fairly standard predictive problem,

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.880
but I think once you delve into it

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.938
you begin to understand some
of the nuances that exist,

00:11:11.938 --> 00:11:14.570
because healthcare data is not as clean

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:16.620
as some of the data that exists

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.183
and is commonly used in machine learning.

00:11:18.183 --> 00:11:21.200
There's many potential biases
that want us to navigate

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.180
in order to ensure

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:26.069
that the underlying model
that's being learned is a true

00:11:26.069 --> 00:11:29.670
and good model of a, a
faithful model rather,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.170
of the outcome that one
cares about predicting.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:34.210
The second example is that
of disease progression,

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.030
which is to say doctors
often collect large cohorts

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:39.260
of patients that course who all suffer

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:40.400
from a common disease.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.380
And so in the context of that problem

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:45.649
one interesting question,
that is how can we

00:11:45.649 --> 00:11:49.010
use this data to
understand diseases better?

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:52.650
Which is to say does
diabetes have predictable

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:56.370
patterns of evolution
within the human population?

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:58.520
And if we were able to
use machine learning to

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.303
discover those predictable
patterns of evolution

00:12:01.303 --> 00:12:05.360
can those then lead doctors to ask new

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.900
and interesting hypothesis

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.100
about how to treat the disease better?

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.610
So really using machine learning

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.060
as a tool for hypothesis discovery.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.900
And what I found during the
course of my own research

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:16.740
is the fact that these problems

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.710
are often require new machine
learning advances as well

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:22.290
in order to be able to
tackle them effectively.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.810
So in the past, I've
worked on new algorithms

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.301
for probabilistic inference
and nonlinear models,

00:12:27.301 --> 00:12:31.320
as well as new techniques to
reduce the sample complexity

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.910
or rather to reduce how data
hungry these models are.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:38.130
Because often in healthcare,
one does not always have access

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:40.153
to millions and millions of samples

00:12:40.153 --> 00:12:42.976
in order to learn these
machine learning models.

00:12:42.976 --> 00:12:45.980
So in the future, I'm
interested in thinking

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:47.380
about how to make use of some

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.508
of the advances that we
have in causal inference

00:12:49.508 --> 00:12:53.240
to build automated clinical
decision making support tools,

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:55.666
as well as thinking
about some of the nuances

00:12:55.666 --> 00:12:58.290
of deploying machine
learning models in hospitals,

00:12:58.290 --> 00:12:59.340
how do we tackle issues

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.640
like the fact that our distribution

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.920
of people changes over time?

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.760
It's not the same people
going into the hospital

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.630
and that has downstream
effects on how well

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.890
any deployed machine
learning algorithm performs.

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:14.030
And finally, to tackle this question

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.160
that you asked of societal benefit.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:17.500
I think there's a few ways

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.760
in which this broad area
can deliver some form

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.220
of societal benefit.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:25.330
And this is particularly
true in the case of Canada

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.350
where there's a, where the
provincial systems are set up

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:30.850
in a way to provide
healthcare for everyone.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:34.810
And so one is in the
context of early prediction

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.430
or risk stratification
for chronic diseases.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.300
There are certain diseases
where if you were to

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:43.730
predict early that a patient
is at risk for continuing

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:46.610
on to develop that disease,
there may be interventions

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:49.711
that one can take and
prevent the development

00:13:49.711 --> 00:13:53.520
of chronic versions of that
disease in that patient.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.520
And so diabetes is one
simple example of it, where

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:03.480
if a patient is put on a
diet and exercise regimen

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.850
prior to the development
of chronic diabetes

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:07.240
they can often stave off some of

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:10.290
the comorbidities that are
associated with that disease.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.910
And so this is a case
where predictive models

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:14.570
can lead to interventions,

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:17.220
which can then lead to
better public health outcomes

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.200
both from a cost perspective,
but more importantly

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.290
from a human health perspective.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:23.550
The second is in the context of developing

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.320
these predictive models and deploying them

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.894
in some of the lower
resource settings where,

00:14:27.894 --> 00:14:31.750
and in doing so reduce
some of the inequality

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.550
that exists in access to healthcare.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.810
So I'll conclude with that

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.590
and I hope I answered
some of the key questions

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:39.423
that you asked.

