00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.200
David, last but not least, your
work is really fascinating.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.550
Tell us about your research

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.480
and its a positive impact on society.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.810
- Thank you so much, it's
a pleasure to be here.

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.850
I work on the applications
of AI to climate change.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.960
So helping society to reduce
greenhouse gas emissions

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:47.690
and also respond to the
effects of climate change.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:49.510
And that means applications in areas

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:52.350
like energy, and
transportation, buildings,

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.540
and urban planning, sustainable
forestry and agriculture,

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:58.720
ecology, and disaster response.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.390
Now climate change is a huge
and multifaceted problem

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:04.190
and AI is not going to solve it.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:07.300
It's not a silver bullet here,
but it can be a powerful tool

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.013
for particular pro particular problems.

00:16:10.013 --> 00:16:14.240
For example, AI can help
distill large amounts

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:17.050
of information to help
policymakers make decisions.

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:18.960
For example, by using satellite imagery,

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:21.710
to automatically monitor
greenhouse gas emissions,

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:25.490
or land use, or monitor ecosystem change.

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.140
It can also help optimize
complicated systems.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.970
So for example, reducing
the energy required to heat

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.790
and cool a building, or
improving the efficiency

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:37.730
of freight transportation
networks, can improve forecasting

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:41.030
for example of agricultural
productivity or food insecurity.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:43.010
And it can also accelerate the process

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.400
of scientific discovery itself
by speeding up the search

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:47.940
for new materials, like those in batteries

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.160
or in photovoltaics.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.010
If you're interested in
the full space of where

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:54.580
AI can be helpful in climate
change, we actually put

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:56.830
out a hundred page paper
with recommendations

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:00.360
that's designed to help guide
this field moving forward

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.180
but let's dive into an example
from my research group.

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:08.570
One of the challenges to using
more solar and wind power

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:11.030
is that the sources of power
vary from moment to moment,

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:12.920
depending upon whether the sun is shining

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:14.350
or the wind is blowing.

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:16.320
That means it's really
difficult to control

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.460
an electrical grid with lots of renewables

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:19.950
because the available
supply of electricity

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:21.600
is always changing.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.670
So basically grid operators need to solve

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.730
a large non-convex optimization problem.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.120
It's called AC Optimal Power Flow,

00:17:28.120 --> 00:17:30.823
or AC-OPF in order to manage the grid.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.580
The existing ways to
do this are very slow.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.560
The problem actually needs to
be solved every few minutes

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:37.460
if you have a lot of
solar and wind energy.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.730
So in practice people have
been radically simplifying

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:41.420
AC-OPF in order to make it more tractable

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.460
and these solutions
are just not very good.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:48.130
My group just released a
deep learning algorithm

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:51.090
that makes it possible to
good and fast solutions,

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:53.337
to AC-OPF by encoding the constraints

00:17:53.337 --> 00:17:55.570
and the problem state
statement, essentially

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.760
providing a guarantee that
the lights won't go out,

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:00.170
that it will satisfy the constraints

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.130
from the electrical grid.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:03.780
And previous deep learning
algorithms on electrical grids

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:05.270
couldn't do that, which as you can imagine

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:07.518
many people much less likely to apply them

00:18:07.518 --> 00:18:09.040
because, you know, if
you're a grid operator

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:11.420
you really want to make
sure the lights don't go.

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:14.410
So our approach actually
not only works for AC-OPF,

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:15.310
but it also works

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.500
for big non-conductive
optimization problems in general.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:19.267
So it's very broadly useful.

00:18:19.267 --> 00:18:21.340
Some of the other projects in my group

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.450
are semi-supervised
remote sensing approaches

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:26.220
for monitoring biodiversity
and ecological change,

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:29.370
and fast approximate simulations
of atmospheric physics

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:32.260
to integrate into large
weather and climate models.

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:35.660
The existing models are great,
but key components of them

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:38.040
like as with the electrical
grid can be very slow

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.320
and machine learning
approximations can speed

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:42.410
some of those components up.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:46.080
Really a key aspect of
our work across problems

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.860
and application domains
is close collaboration

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:50.710
with domain experts and other stakeholders

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.850
like the Montreal Insectarium,

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.030
and Environment and Climate Change Canada,

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:56.510
to ensure that the work is guided

00:18:56.510 --> 00:19:00.109
by the societal benefits
and is deployed impactfully.

00:19:00.109 --> 00:19:02.840
And as Elissa mentioned, I'm
also co-founder and chair

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.870
of the Climate Change AI
initiative, which facilitates work

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:08.120
