contexts that the model

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:27.160
may be considered to be applied in.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.070
So what's relevant in hospital one

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:30.950
for predicting a patient outcome,

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:33.510
may simply not be
relevant in hospital two,

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:34.750
because the way they do things

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:37.370
in terms of their processes
is fundamentally different.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:39.980
So I think in the context of healthcare,

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:41.950
there's two key considerations

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:45.655
for thinking about responsibly

00:25:45.655 --> 00:25:49.130
I think important to start with at least,

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:50.470
and there's many, many more.

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:53.420
And so the first is working
with domain experts.

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:58.170
I think there's absolutely
no substitute for working

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:01.700
with a domain expert in
a area like healthcare

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:05.260
because he or she is the one who's worked

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.930
with the data every day of their career.

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:12.070
And they arguably know
about any limitations

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:15.550
that the data may have and
can think through the impacts

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.050
of those limitations on
the downstream performance

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:20.530
of any model that's being developed.

00:26:20.530 --> 00:26:23.010
And so the second key
consideration I'd say is

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:24.770
monitoring the performance of the model

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:26.800
in any deployed solution.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:29.270
So I think, you know

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:32.800
this notion of responsible
AI is not, it's not a one-off

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:36.543
it doesn't stop when the
model is done training.

00:26:36.543 --> 00:26:38.670
It's a sort of a commitment

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:43.670
to continue to see how the model
performs in a live scenario

00:26:43.789 --> 00:26:47.400
and monitor it for
unintended consequences.

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:48.990
And what I wanna point out here is

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:52.150
that these unintended
consequences need not be

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:54.758
from what the model is predicting alone.

00:26:54.758 --> 00:26:57.487
They could be much bigger

00:26:57.487 --> 00:27:00.110
implied consequences that come about

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:02.290
as the model is used in practice.

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:04.480
So the ground that in a concrete example,

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:08.340
you can think of a
prediction tool that say

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:12.450
predicts whether or not a
patient should be recommended

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:17.084
for a second opinion for in
the context of say a disease.

00:27:17.084 --> 00:27:20.470
And so if this model
is really, really good

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:24.050
at this prediction task,
what essentially happens is

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:27.240
that you have a much
larger volume of people

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:29.970
who are now going to
doctors and specialists

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:32.896
for second opinions, but we
have a finite set of resources,

00:27:32.896 --> 00:27:35.290
our finite set of specialists,

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:38.420
who may not have the capacity
to handle that volume.

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.110
And so this is an unintended consequence

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:42.330
not due to the failure of the model,

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.400
but rather due to the systemic nature

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:47.470
of the healthcare system that
this model is deployed in.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.920
And so I I'd say that, like,
it's important to think

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:53.810
about both the micro and
the macro scale consequences

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.630
of deployed solutions or
machine learning In this context

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:57.470
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:27:57.470 --> 00:28:00.404
And both of those examples
that you gave, remind me of

00:28:00.404 --> 00:28:03.780
especially in a healthcare
context, this idea of, you know

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:04.940
the humans in the loop, right?

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.850
Like, so keeping the clinicians very close

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:09.890
to the development of the
machine learning algorithms

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:11.500
and tools that are being deployed,

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:15.000
but then, you know, we as practitioners

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.010
following the evolution of our models

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:20.560
as they are deployed and
out there in the world.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:21.730
So that's great.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.060
How about you Mo, tell us about, you know,

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:26.611
the considerations around responsible AI

00:28:26.611 --> 00:28:28.393
when we're talking about robotics.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:30.980
- Yeah, in fact, I
mean, even though health

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:31.813
and robotics says

00:28:31.813 --> 00:28:33.700
it sounds like kind of different areas,

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:35.690
I actually think that
there's a lot in common.

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:38.470
So one of the things that we
really worry about is like

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.560
if an AI makes a error in judgment,

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:43.670
it's not just some misclassified image,

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:46.610
but in both robotics and
health, there's, I mean,

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:51.170
there's immediate physical
consequences to people.

00:28:51.170 --> 00:28:53.990
So of course I think safety
and sort of reliability

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.380
of the systems is
really, really important.

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:58.160
And I think it's actually
one of the bottlenecks

00:28:58.160 --> 00:28:59.660
that we need to get through,

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:02.940
in order to kind of advance
the field of robotics

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.083
in everyday life.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:07.580
I think a lot of these
keywords I'm gonna mention

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:09.760
you probably have heard like
interpretability, right?

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:12.200
I think robots will make mistakes

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:13.220
and hopefully not too many,

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:14.680
but they will make some mistakes.

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:16.800
And when they do, we
need to understand why

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:18.363
and systematically kind of,

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:22.333
sort of improve the areas
that they are weak at.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:26.270
I think one, one thing that
I am pretty interested in

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:30.330
is to have robots realize
when they should be unsure

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:31.230
of themselves.

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:35.260
So if you have like a,
if you have a driver

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:39.380
let's say a human driver
or a human doctor, right?

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:41.540
Especially during training, they...

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:45.020
Well, training is now kind
of an overloaded term now.

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:46.640
So what people know

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.640
that people know when
they should be unsure

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:50.570
and maybe when they
should ask help, right?

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:52.420
This is something that I
don't really think robots

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:54.020
are, can do yet.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:56.470
And hopefully they will
be able to do this later.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:59.660
One thing that is also
very important for robotics

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:02.270
is how can we learn safely, right?

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:05.540
One of the ways I'm learning
safely is in simulation.

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:10.490
And so can we develop
methods that can robustly

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:13.450
transfer behavior, learned in
simulation to the real world?

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:16.103
So this is a very active area of research.

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:21.660
So in the end, I think one of
the really more complex things

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:24.423
that I wanna consider
is really people's past.

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:29.860
So suppose we make these
systems very technically robust,

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:33.730
technically speaking, they are
very, very, let's say safe.

00:30:33.730 --> 00:30:35.370
Will people still trust them, right?

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:39.050
A lot of people, a lot of
times we've seen user studies

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:40.850
where the robot is behaving optimally,

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:44.330
but people feel that, feel
that the robot is acting weird.

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.260
And even though the robot
is doing probably better

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:49.230
than humans, humans don't
necessarily trust the robots.

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.310
So in terms of trust,

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.463
I think it's a very very typical topic.

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:58.050
I think one thing that we
would have to do as a community

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:01.070
is to make sure we
educate everyone, right?

00:31:01.070 --> 00:31:03.630
Not just the students,
but the entire community

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:06.930
including people who have already
been working in AI, right?

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.604
We need to understand we can choose to be,

00:31:09.604 --> 00:31:13.450
we can choose to trust
AI, or maybe be skeptical,

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:17.030
but the trust or skepticism,
it needs to be informed.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.360
And I think as a community

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:22.522
this is a very important
thing in the long-term.

00:31:22.522 --> 00:31:24.897
- Yeah, and any thoughts Mo
on how we should do that?

00:31:24.897 --> 00:31:26.910
You know, you're talking specifically,

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:29.260
I think about the research community,

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:31.867
I and my colleagues at CIFAR
had been thinking about

00:31:31.867 --> 00:31:34.329
the public and engaging
the public around AI.

00:31:34.329 --> 00:31:36.333
Do you think we have to do both?

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:41.790
- Well, obviously I'm kind of
focused more on the education

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.810
of students, like the
graduate students, as well as

00:31:43.810 --> 00:31:47.130
the undergraduates, just
kinda running into the

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:48.910
maybe like advice for students.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:50.790
I think ...

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:51.670
- Go ahead.

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:53.920
- I would say like, we need
to learn the fundamentals

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:54.753
properly.

00:31:54.753 --> 00:31:56.070
There are no shortcuts, right?

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:58.060
So a lot of times when we think of AI,

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:01.290
we think of perhaps
downloading some toolbox

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:04.450
that is, that makes doing
research convenient,

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:07.437
but those should be more
like a jumpstart, right?

00:32:07.437 --> 00:32:10.360
There's still no shortcut
in learning the math,

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:11.870
learning the fundamentals,

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:14.560
and Canada being one of the places

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:16.830
where we really value
fundamental research,

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:18.550
this is especially important.

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:23.550
And I would say this
extends not beyond students,

00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:25.220
this is also for everyone involved.

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:28.220
I've talked to many of my
friends who are actually

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:30.870
well-educated and they are aware of AI,

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:32.990
but I would say that in general,

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:35.270
I really hope to see people understand

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:37.710
the fundamental fundamentals
a little bit more.

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:38.580
- Great.
- And maybe, yeah.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:42.350
Maybe more events like this,
in fact, that could help.

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:45.570
- Yeah, that's part of why we
do it, for sure, absolutely.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:47.990
Okay, I'm gonna take a few
questions from the audience now.

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:49.810
We've had lots of great questions come in

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:51.300
and I want to share one with you

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.920
that's particularly been up-voted

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:55.439
by a number of individuals

00:32:55.439 --> 00:32:58.960
and people are putting their
thumbs up for this one.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:00.890
It's coming in from the Ashwell,.

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:03.360
So it's, you know, it's,
it's a loaded question.

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.380
What should happen to make sure

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:08.050
AI for social good
applications are developed

00:33:08.050 --> 00:33:09.800
even when the profit motive

00:33:09.800 --> 00:33:12.950
is not sufficient to motivate companies?

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:17.520
So, you know, how do we
make sure that, you know

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:20.500
AI for Good applications
are really a priority

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:21.850
for us as a research community,

00:33:21.850 --> 00:33:25.090
a research and innovation
community when the private sector

00:33:25.090 --> 00:33:27.220
and their profit motive is not,

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:29.760
is not high enough for
them to be involved.

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:31.743
Thoughts on, on that balance.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:34.190
Anybody wanna chip?
- Well,

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:35.460
I'd like to start by saying

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:37.993
it helps to have a monetary
incentive obviously,

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:40.943
but when that isn't there,

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:45.200
I think in the research
community can fairly easily

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:48.070
be convinced that this is an
important problem to look at.

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:51.270
I think the community
that needs to be convinced

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:55.015
is the industry, the business,
and the general public.

00:33:55.015 --> 00:33:58.930
Sometimes they're just not
aware and possibly even,

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:00.940
I mean I believe in a
multi-pronged approach

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:05.520
so possibly even, sort of the
regulatory or policy bodies.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:08.538
One, you know, analogy I like to use is,

00:34:08.538 --> 00:34:11.120
you know, industries like telecom,

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:15.160
where I worked for many
years ago have standards

00:34:15.160 --> 00:34:17.620
that their protocols must abide by.

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:19.614
And that's important for the communication

00:34:19.614 --> 00:34:22.980
but for areas where you don't need that,

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:25.151
you still have certain guidelines.

00:34:25.151 --> 00:34:26.810
Like many years ago

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:31.230
the consumer protection
agencies were very important

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.940
and their work led to
things like having labels

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:35.620
on clothing irons that say

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:37.920
don't iron while you're
wearing your clothes.

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:40.480
So, and you know, no one's saying

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:42.120
that clothing iron shouldn't be produced

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:43.630
and shouldn't exist,

00:34:43.630 --> 00:34:47.400
but the harm that can be
produced from it was recognized.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:51.600
So in the same way, I think
for AI products and services,

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:53.490
I think we all agree that
they're very important

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:58.490
and impactful, but the harm
that they may result in

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.760
is not properly recognized.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.320
And so maybe, you know,
we need something like,

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:06.828
something like these
consumer protection agencies

00:35:06.828 --> 00:35:09.260
that allow people to be educated

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:12.410
about the harms and how
they can be safeguarded.

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:14.550
So if the people are then aware of this

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:18.090
then they can push using their, you know,

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:20.060
where they spend their money on,

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:21.890
they can push the industry or businesses

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:23.550
in certain directions.

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:24.383
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:35:24.383 --> 00:35:26.720
I'm reminded again, to
go back to healthcare,

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:30.410
where it's, you know a highly
regulated sector in Canada

00:35:30.410 --> 00:35:33.330
and the US and both the
FDA and health Canada,

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:36.760
you know, actively and carefully examine

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:39.180
machine applications very closely

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:41.260
before they're implemented
within, you know,

00:35:41.260 --> 00:35:44.043
within health systems and
they are, it's happening,

00:35:44.043 --> 00:35:46.810
and so that's, that's
definitely the right direction.

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:47.836
Anything else

00:35:47.836 --> 00:35:49.946
on public private sector?
- Hey can I?

00:35:49.946 --> 00:35:51.153
- Go ahead, Dave.

00:35:51.153 --> 00:35:52.670
- I can step in a bit.

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:57.670
So I think that one
component is using our voices

00:35:58.040 --> 00:36:01.230
not necessarily in a research capacity,

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:04.720
but in the capacity of thought leaders

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:09.720
to add to the conversation
about what best policies

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:12.823
and best practices are.

00:36:14.340 --> 00:36:17.160
we have, we have leadership roles

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:19.720
that don't involve necessarily

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:21.390
coming up with a new algorithm.

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:24.480
People, it is important for the community

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:26.810
to use its voice in that way.

00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:29.358
And then I think another component of this

00:36:29.358 --> 00:36:32.830
and then that does involve
informing stakeholders

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:34.620
of what the capabilities of systems are

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.570
and what they can do to best incentivize,

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:39.310
incentivize meaningful impact.

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:42.960
And that also means informing
ourselves what that means.

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:46.360
But I think another
component is remembering

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:50.560
that research needs to be
scoped with impact in mind

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:51.560
from the get-go.

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:52.420
It's not a question of

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:56.600
I wrote a great paper
with a great project,

00:36:56.600 --> 00:36:59.860
and now I'm going to look for
a way to make it impactful.

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:02.970
Oh, wait, I don't see a path to impact.

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.554
This is why one should work
with stakeholders from,

00:37:05.554 --> 00:37:09.130
from like the initial scoping of a project

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:10.500
and the development of the project,

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:12.240
in addition to the
deployment of the project,

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:13.750
because once you get to deployment

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.440
you haven't scoped it
together with the people

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:16.760
who will ultimately be using it,

00:37:16.760 --> 00:37:17.720
it's too late.

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:19.160
There are ton of opportunities

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:22.030
where the incentives are aligned right now

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:25.350
and making sure that one
is working with the people

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:28.808
who will be deploying your
projects can help you ensure

00:37:28.808 --> 00:37:32.190
you are working in a space
where you will be able to

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:34.433
have the impacts that you want.

00:37:34.433 --> 00:37:37.055
- That's great, David, thank you for that.

00:37:37.055 --> 00:37:38.760
And it is actually your response speaks

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:41.235
to one of the other questions that came in

00:37:41.235 --> 00:37:43.980
that reads "What do you see as the role of

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:46.770
individual researchers
versus the organization

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:48.620
or external players like governments,

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:51.670
in ensuring that AI is used
in socially beneficial ways."

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:53.860
So I wanted sort of share that one

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:55.470
with the other members of the panel.

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:59.660
What do you see as your role
as individual researchers in

00:37:59.660 --> 00:38:03.802
you know, being a voice
around responsible use of AI?

00:38:03.802 --> 00:38:05.270
Anybody?

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:07.750
- Yeah, I can, I can chime in on this.

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:12.750
I think as individual researchers,
a, we serve two roles.

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:17.018
One is education, which
is educating students

00:38:17.018 --> 00:38:19.786
be undergraduate or the graduate level,

00:38:19.786 --> 00:38:23.620
and teaching them to

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:25.580
create new algorithms,

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:27.200
and machine learning, and robotics,

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:29.050
and healthcare, and climate science.

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:32.460
And it's, I think that
educational component of it

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:35.300
is a big part of the role that we can play

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:39.280
in order to make sure that
the effort that we put in

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:43.420
is multiplied by the
students who learned from us.

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:46.670
And as long as we in, incorporate in them

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:50.620
a sense of responsibility,
a sense of values,

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:54.040
and in how to ensure
that these applications

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:55.620
are used responsibly,

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:58.200
I think that's one of
the most impactful ways

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.470
that we can help,

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:04.710
because ultimately that's who
we spend a lot of time with,

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:05.543
the students.

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:09.430
The second way is I
think I agree with David

00:39:09.430 --> 00:39:12.080
is having our voices heard

00:39:12.080 --> 00:39:15.350
in a variety of different scenarios,

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:19.210
and ensuring that the ideas we put forth

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:24.210
are ones that are consistent
with these notions of safety

00:39:25.342 --> 00:39:27.458
and responsibility and responsibly

00:39:27.458 --> 00:39:31.620
using the more technical
approaches that we develop.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:32.997
- Great, thank you.

00:39:32.997 --> 00:39:35.359
Okay, we're, I've got two
minutes left on the clock,

00:39:35.359 --> 00:39:36.509
or maybe even less.

00:39:36.509 --> 00:39:39.330
So we're gonna switch to
sort of final thoughts

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:41.750
and I'd love to just
have each of you give us

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:45.700
in sort of 30 seconds, let's
call it 20 seconds or less.

00:39:45.700 --> 00:39:49.120
Tell us, you know, your best
advice for graduate students

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:52.360
interested in your particular
area of machine learning.

00:39:52.360 --> 00:39:54.480
What should they be doing now to prepare

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:57.600
for their future careers
in academia and industry.

00:39:57.600 --> 00:39:58.893
Mo we'll start with you.

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:01.320
- Yeah, as I mentioned earlier,

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:03.300
we need to learn the
fundamentals properly, right?

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:05.980
No short cuts, we need to, in robotics

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:09.320
we need theory, how the
theory translates to code,

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:12.440
and how code translates to robots moving

00:40:12.440 --> 00:40:13.800
in the physical world, right?

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:16.010
And don't be afraid to collaborate

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:19.540
with other faculty members
other than your supervisor

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:21.200
and internships are great.

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:24.390
So just to get all the expertise,
all the all the expertise

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:25.850
wherever you can get.

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:26.737
- That's great, thank you.

00:40:26.737 --> 00:40:29.717
Nidhi, your best advice
for graduate students?

00:40:29.717 --> 00:40:33.630
- So I think my topic is
necessarily multidisciplinary

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:38.045
and necessarily has a clear and
immediate impact on society.

00:40:38.045 --> 00:40:42.770
So I would suggest that
they make themselves aware

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:47.022
of actual, real impacts, like
real examples that, you know

00:40:47.022 --> 00:40:51.220
journalists have investigated
on where privacy breech

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:55.510
has occurred, or where fairness
or bias questions arise.

00:40:55.510 --> 00:40:56.760
I think that's important.

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:58.480
And also second what most said,

00:40:58.480 --> 00:40:59.980
which is understanding the fundamentals

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:02.010
because things like privacy and fairness

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:04.550
you know, they're,
sometimes they're even hard

00:41:04.550 --> 00:41:07.050
to intuitively say what they mean to us,

00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:09.720
and they may be even harder
in a mathematical way

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:12.583
to define what they are,
and that's really important.

00:41:12.583 --> 00:41:15.170
Do we know, we need to be
rigorous about these definitions

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:19.400
and about how we derive
these in order to then

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:23.490
create algorithms that, that
mitigate these problems.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:27.050
And so the fundamentals are
important and multidisciplinary

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:29.320
you know, don't be afraid to talk to

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:32.610
people in law, or in philosophy,

00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:35.250
to get a more well-rounded understanding.

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:36.990
- Absolutely, Rahul over to you.

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:39.180
Best advice for grad students.

00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:42.150
- Yeah, I think there's
two pieces of advice

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:43.710
that I would really have.

00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:45.560
I would echo most thoughts on

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:47.740
and focus on foundational knowledge.

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:51.870
So when you think about classes,

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:53.940
don't think of them as

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:57.220
I wanna learn the most
advanced topic first,

00:41:57.220 --> 00:41:59.960
I think it's much, it's
very much in your interest

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.610
to pick the more foundational topics

00:42:02.610 --> 00:42:05.867
on the algebra, optimization,
stochastic processes,

00:42:05.867 --> 00:42:10.110
and really this is because
machine learning is fundamentally

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:13.249
just a melting pot of
different sub-disciplines,

00:42:13.249 --> 00:42:15.544
and it has always been that way.

00:42:15.544 --> 00:42:20.544
And so being very good and
having strong foundations

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:24.100
is crucial to any future
success in the sub-area.

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:26.670
And the second thing that I'd say is in

00:42:26.670 --> 00:42:29.203
at least what I found when
I was studying is the fact

00:42:29.203 --> 00:42:32.690
that I think patterns
are sort of universal.

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:36.480
And I think if you learn to
recognize patterns that exist

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:39.820
in different classes that you're taking,

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:42.330
and have a conceptual framework

00:42:42.330 --> 00:42:44.977
by which you can learn to manipulate them,

00:42:44.977 --> 00:42:46.960
that's an incredible skill to have,

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:49.890
because at the end of the day,

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:52.910
a lot of ideas are rediscovered and reused

00:42:52.910 --> 00:42:55.860
in different sub-fields and
being able to flexibly move

00:42:55.860 --> 00:43:00.860
within fields and describe how
one idea relates to another

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:02.730
in a completely separate arena,

00:43:02.730 --> 00:43:05.200
it will only strengthen
sort of the mental models

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:07.830
you have of the world
and ultimately lead to

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:12.400
more interesting kinds of research ideas.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:14.653
- Great advice, David, last word to you.

00:43:16.090 --> 00:43:18.633
- Yeah, so to echo what
other people have said.

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:23.300
learn the fundamentals, but
not just machine learning,

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:26.029
learn the fundamentals of whatever areas

00:43:26.029 --> 00:43:30.033
you're looking to integrate
with machine learning,

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:32.910
and go in with humility.

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:36.660
Recognize that each of these
other areas of application

00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:40.470
has decades or centuries
of great work in it.

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:42.300
And machine learning is
not suddenly gonna solve

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:44.257
all those problems,

00:43:44.257 --> 00:43:47.090
or if it does solve
some of those problems,

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:47.923
it's going to do that only

00:43:47.923 --> 00:43:50.840
by integrating with all of
that existing knowledge,

00:43:50.840 --> 00:43:52.670
and by really carefully
working with people

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:55.640
who are expert in those areas.

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:58.670
And if you want to have any
impact, start from the problem,

00:43:58.670 --> 00:44:00.600
whether it's a theoretical
or an applied problem,

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:01.680
start from the problem

00:44:01.680 --> 00:44:06.470
and let the tools that you use
evolve to match the problem.

00:44:06.470 --> 00:44:08.157
Don't go in assuming that the,

00:44:08.157 --> 00:44:10.650
the fanciest newest tools are best.

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.060
And then paradoxically
often that is actually

00:44:13.060 --> 00:44:14.960
what will lead to foundational innovation.

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:17.150
That is what will lead
to the breakthroughs in

00:44:17.150 --> 00:44:19.010
from an algorithmic perspective too,

00:44:19.010 --> 00:44:21.343
is really being driven
by what people need.

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:24.360
- This is great advice

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:26.540
and it's been such a
fabulous conversation.

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:27.990
I hate to have to cut it off

00:44:27.990 --> 00:44:30.730
but I'm afraid that is all
the time we have for today.

00:44:30.730 --> 00:44:32.980
So thanks so much to all of
you, all of our panelists

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:35.760
for sharing their insights
and experience experiences.

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:38.260
We're really thrilled to have
you as part of the candidacy

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:40.890
for AI Chairs program and
thanks to the audience

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:43.650
for great questions and
discussion over the chat.

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:45.539
It's been fantastic.

00:44:45.539 --> 00:44:48.539
